INT. GRAIN SILO - NIGHT

Dark. A circular concrete shaft. A single flashlight beam jitters over rusted ladders.

IMOGEN TSAI, 20s, clutches the flashlight. KATYA BRANDVOLD, 20s, presses against her back. RUFUS DELACOURT, 30s, kneels at a hatch. SILAS OKOYE, 40s, leans on his knees and wheezes.

IMOGEN
Did you hear that?

KATYA
Don't say that.

RUFUS
It's the wind.

SILAS
There is no wind in a silo.

KATYA
Then what is it?

IMOGEN
Quiet.

Something drags above them. A slow scrape on concrete.

RUFUS
It's on the ladder.

SILAS
It's on our ladder.

KATYA
Imogen, give me the light.

IMOGEN
No.

KATYA
Give me the light, or I swear to God...

IMOGEN
Katya, stop. If you move, it knows where we are.

SILAS
It already knows.

RUFUS
Then why hasn't it come down?

KATYA
Because it's waiting for one of us to scream.

IMOGEN
I'm not going to scream.

The flashlight flickers out. Somewhere above, a long, patient breath.
